{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.8,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["Q2"],
      "pins": ["Q2.1", "Q2.3"],
      "nets": ["PWM", "Q1_gate (R1/VCC node)"],
      "observed": "Q2 (BC817) pin 1 (Base) is wired upward to Q1's gate node (shared with R1 10 kΩ pull-up to VCC), and Q2 pin 3 (Collector) is wired rightward through R2 1 kΩ to the PWM net. The base and collector are swapped relative to the standard BC817 pinout (pin 1 = Base, pin 2 = Emitter, pin 3 = Collector).",
      "expected": "In a common-emitter NPN driver for a high-side PMOS, the PWM signal should enter Q2's Base (pin 1) through R2, and Q2's Collector (pin 3) should connect to Q1's Gate (with R1 pulling the gate to VCC when Q2 is off). The schematic has these two pins reversed.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Page 1: Q2 symbol shows pin 1 at top connected by a vertical wire to Q1 pin 3 (gate); pin 3 at right connected through R2 1K to the PWM label; pin 2 at bottom to GND."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Page 1: R1 10K connects the Q1-gate / Q2-pin-1 node to VCC."}
      ],
      "impact": "Q2's base is permanently pulled high by R1 to VCC, so Q2 is always ON, but its collector is on the PWM net, not on Q1's gate. Q1's gate is held high by R1 regardless of the PWM signal, so the PMOS never turns on. The fan never receives switched power through Q1; the PWM speed-control path is completely broken.",
      "confidence": 0.82
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["U1"],
      "pins": ["U1.1"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VCC"],
      "observed": "U1 (LM1117-5.0) pin 1 (VIN) is connected to the +5V net — the same net as U1 pin 2 (VOUT). The VCC net, which originates from the J5 PWR connector (pin 2), is not connected to U1 VIN.",
      "expected": "The LM1117-5.0 regulator should take the unregulated VCC input from J5 at its VIN pin and produce a regulated +5V at VOUT, supplying the microcontrollers (U2/U3 pin 8), the I2C connector J1, the POT connector J3, and the RST pull-up R3.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Page 2: U1 pin 1 wire goes up to label '+5V' (with C2 100 nF to GND); U1 pin 2 wire goes up to label '+5V'. Both input and output are on the same net."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Page 1: J5 pin 2 carries the VCC label; C1 100 nF, JP1 pin 2, R6, and R1 are on VCC. No wire or label connects VCC to U1 pin 1."}
      ],
      "impact": "The regulator is bypassed (VIN = VOUT), so the +5V rail has no source from the main power input J5/VCC. Unless +5V is supplied externally through J6 (I2C_PWR) or J1, the microcontrollers and all +5V-referenced logic are unpowered and the board does not function.",
      "confidence": 0.65
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Two wiring anomalies were found. First, Q2 (BC817) has its base and collector swapped, preventing the PMOS fan switch from ever turning on. Second, U1 (LM1117-5.0) has its input tied to the +5V output net instead of the VCC power-input net, leaving the regulated rail without a source from the main power connector."
}

